-- title:
--   Inertia with trivial tame character fixes q-th roots of unity
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a natural number, assumed prime, let $P$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` lying over $q$ in the sense that the image of $q$ in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ belongs to `P.nonunits`, i.e. $q \in P$ but $q$ is not a unit of $P$, and let $\pi \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ satisfy $\pi^{q^2-1} = q$. Let $\tau$ be a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying in the inertia subgroup of $P$, understood as the image in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ of the inertia subgroup of the decomposition subgroup of $P$ under the inclusion of that decomposition subgroup. Assume that the tame character of $P$ at $\pi$ takes the value $1$ at $\tau$, where this character sends an automorphism $\sigma$ to the residue class of $\sigma\pi/\pi$ in the residue field of $P$ whenever $\sigma\pi/\pi \in P$ and to $0$ otherwise. Then for every $\zeta \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\zeta^q = 1$ one has $\tau\zeta = \zeta$.
--
--   This is the statement that the level-$(q^2-1)$ tame character at a uniformiser $\pi$ with $\pi^{q^2-1}=q$ detects the action on $q$-th roots of unity: the cyclotomic field $\mathbb{Q}_q(\zeta_q)$ sits inside the tame extension generated by $\pi$, so an inertia element fixing $\pi$ fixes $\mu_q$. It is used in the analysis of the Galois action on the full-level modular curve and its reduction, for instance by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.arithmeticGalois_mul_ofAlgAut_levelAutBar_of_tameCharacter_eq_one`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.arithmeticGalois_mul_ofAlgAut_levelAutBar_of_tameCharacter_eq_one).
